#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722762 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xe^{xy}=e^2+2e-ye^x.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Bring all variable terms to the left and set\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=xe^{xy}+ye^x.\n$$\n\nThe curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=e^2+2e.$ The point is on it because $f(1,2)=e^2+2e.$\n\nWe need $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j.$ Apply the product and chain rules before substituting the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+ye^x,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=x^2e^{xy}+e^x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $f_x,$ differentiating $xe^{xy}$ contributes both $e^{xy}$ and $xy e^{xy};$ in $f_y,$ the chain rule contributes the second factor of $x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=e^2+2e^2+2e=3e^2+2e,\\\\\nf_y(1,2)&=e^2+e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA tangent vector is consequently\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,2)=(e^2+e)\\mathbf i-(3e^2+2e)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero since both components are positive. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1,2)\\cdot\\mathbf t(1,2)\n&=(3e^2+2e)(e^2+e)\\\\\n&\\quad -(e^2+e)(3e^2+2e)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the vector is tangent. Any nonzero scalar multiple is also tangent; none of the other listed vectors has this direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722770 :question/problem "At which point on the curve $x^2+y=4$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf j=\\langle 0,1\\rangle$?\n\nUse $F(x,y)=x^2+y$ and the tangent-vector formula $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$. Perpendicular vectors have dot product zero." :question/worked-solution "The curve is the level curve $F(x,y)=4$. Its partial derivatives are $F_x=2x$ and $F_y=1$, so a tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y)=\\langle 1,-2x\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor this vector to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf j$, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot\\mathbf j&=0\\\\\n-2x&=0\\\\\nx&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=0$ into the original curve: $0^2+y=4$, so $y=4$. Thus the point is $(0,4)$. Here $\\mathbf t=\\langle 1,0\\rangle$ is nonzero and has zero dot product with $\\mathbf j$. Every other listed point lies on the curve, but has $x\\ne0$ and hence a nonzero dot product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722773 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $x^2y-xy^2=2$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j$?\n\nChoose the option giving all such points." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=x^2y-xy^2$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=2$. Compute the partial derivatives and a tangent vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2xy-y^2, &F_y&=x^2-2xy,\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle x^2-2xy,\\,y^2-2xy\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given vector is $\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot(\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j)&=0\\\\\n(x^2-2xy)-(y^2-2xy)&=0\\\\\nx^2-y^2&=0\\\\\n(x-y)(x+y)&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth branches must be checked against the original curve.\n\nIf $y=x$, then $x^2x-xx^2=0$, which cannot equal $2$. This branch supplies no points.\n\nIf $y=-x$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2(-x)-x(-x)^2&=2\\\\\n-2x^3&=2\\\\\nx^3&=-1\\\\\nx&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $y=1$. At $(-1,1)$ the original expression equals $1-(-1)=2$, and $\\mathbf t=\\langle 3,3\\rangle$ is nonzero with $\\langle 3,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle 1,-1\\rangle=0$. The answer is $(-1,1)$ only. The other points in the answer choices also lie on the curve. At $(2,1)$, the tangent vector is $\\langle 0,-3\\rangle$, whose dot product with $\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$ is $3$. At $(-1,-2)$, it is $\\langle -3,0\\rangle$, whose dot product is $-3$. Neither point qualifies."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722785 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $y^2+2xy=x^2+18$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $F(x,y)=y^2+2xy-x^2$. The curve is $F(x,y)=18$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2y-2x, &F_y&=2y+2x,\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle 2y+2x,\\,2x-2y\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity to $\\mathbf j$ requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot\\mathbf j&=0\\\\\n2x-2y&=0\\\\\ny&=x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=x$ into the original curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+2x^2&=x^2+18\\\\\n2x^2&=18\\\\\nx^2&=9\\\\\nx&=3\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y=x$, the points are $(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$. The tangent vectors there are $\\langle 12,0\\rangle$ and $\\langle -12,0\\rangle$, both nonzero and perpendicular to $\\mathbf j$. The other listed points also lie on the curve, but fail $y=x$. Select exactly $(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722859 :question/problem "Differentiate $\\mathbf f(t)+2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f(t)&=\\langle t^2,\\,3t-2,\\,4\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf g(t)&=\\langle t,\\,1-t^2,\\,2t\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The sum and constant multiple rules give\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f+2\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)=\\mathbf f\\prime(t)+2\\mathbf g\\prime(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f\\prime(t)&=\\langle 2t,\\,3,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf g\\prime(t)&=\\langle 1,\\,-2t,\\,2\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply every component of $\\mathbf g\\prime(t)$ by $2$, then add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf f+2\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)&=\\langle 2t,\\,3,\\,0\\rangle+2\\langle 1,\\,-2t,\\,2\\rangle \\\\ &=\\langle 2t+2,\\,3-4t,\\,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722862 :question/problem "For $t>-1$, find $(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)$ in component form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f(t)&=\\langle \\sqrt{t+1},\\,\\ln(t+1),\\,\\cos(2t)\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf g(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t+1},\\,e^{2t},\\,\\sin(2t)\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Keep the constant coefficients while differentiating:\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)=2\\mathbf f\\prime(t)-\\mathbf g\\prime(t).\n$$\n\nThe component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f\\prime(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,-2\\sin(2t)\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf g\\prime(t)&=\\langle -\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2},\\,2e^{2t},\\,2\\cos(2t)\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors of $2$ in the exponential and trigonometric derivatives come from differentiating their inner argument $2t$. Now scale and subtract component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\mathbf f\\prime(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,\\frac{2}{t+1},\\,-4\\sin(2t)\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting $\\mathbf g\\prime(t)$ gives $(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{t+1}-2e^{2t} \\\\ -4\\sin(2t)-2\\cos(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe first component contains a plus sign because the reciprocal derivative is negative and is being subtracted. The stated domain keeps both the logarithm and all derivative components defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722864 :question/problem "Find $(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(1)$, where, for $t>0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{t+3} \\\\ \\cos(t-1) \\\\ \\ln t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf g(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}t^2 \\\\ t^3 \\\\ e^{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate before substituting the evaluation point. The sum and constant multiple rules give\n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(t)=2\\mathbf f\\prime(t)-\\mathbf g\\prime(t).\n$$\n\nComponent by component,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f\\prime(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+3}} \\\\ -\\sin(t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf g\\prime(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 3t^2 \\\\ e^{t-1}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf f\\prime(1)&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf g\\prime(1)&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombine the derivative values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\mathbf f-\\mathbf g)\\prime(1)&=2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac12 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\\\ 0-3 \\\\ 2-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722877 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 x + y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$, simplify the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=8 t+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[8 t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=16 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=16 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (16 \\pi)=16 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722883 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x - y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$, simplify the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=- 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 6\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=6 t+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[6 t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (12 \\pi)=12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722897 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 2 x - 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- 18 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-18 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t=1$, simplify the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int -18\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 18 t+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 18 t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 36 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 36 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (- 36 \\pi)=- 36 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722905 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x - y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+22 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 22 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=11 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[11 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(6 \\pi) + (0) + (- 6 \\pi)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722907 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x - y)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 x + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 4 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=4 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 4 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 8 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 8 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[4 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=8 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=8 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 8 \\pi) + (0) + (8 \\pi)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722926 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x - y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x - y^{3})\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=- \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1-2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3-4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=t + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- \\frac{3 t}{8} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{32}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[t + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=2 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- \\frac{3 t}{8} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{4} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{32}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(2 \\pi) + \\left(- \\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722931 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^{3})\\,\\mathbf i+(2 x + y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1+2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3+4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=2 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(6 \\pi) + (0) + (2 \\pi)=8 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722933 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- x + y^{3})\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=8 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(8 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1-2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3-4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- \\frac{\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 8 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- t - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 2 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- \\frac{\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 2 \\pi) + (0) + (6 \\pi)=4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722936 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x^{3} + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReduce the fourth power by applying the cosine double-angle identity twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^4t&=\\left(\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2\\right)^2\\\\&=\\frac14\\left(1+2\\cos(2t)+\\frac{1+\\cos(4t)}2\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{3+4\\cos(2t)+\\cos(4t)}8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 8 \\cos^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 3 t - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - \\frac{\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=4 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 6 \\pi) + (0) + (4 \\pi)=- 2 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722939 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x + e^{y})\\,\\mathbf i+(e^{x})\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=2 e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor the exponential term in $\\sin t$, use $u=2\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=2\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nFor the exponential term in $\\cos t$, use $v=1\\cos t$, $\\mathrm{d}v=-1\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 2 e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 2 t - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 4 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- e^{\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- e\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- e\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[e^{2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=1\\\\&\\quad-\\left(1\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 4 \\pi) + (0) + (0)=- 4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722940 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x y + x)\\,\\mathbf i+(- 2 x^{2} - y)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 32 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 32 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-200 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\sin t$, use $u=\\cos t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=-\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 200 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=\\frac{200 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[\\frac{200 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=\\frac{200}{3}\\\\&\\quad-\\left(\\frac{200}{3}\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 t + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (12 \\pi) + (- 12 \\pi)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722954 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x^{2} + x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2 y + 1)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1,\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+18 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\sin^2t=\\frac{1-\\cos(2t)}2$, $\\cos^3t=(1-\\sin^2t)\\cos t$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 18 \\cos^{3}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} + 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} + 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[3 t + \\frac{3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=6 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=6 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=-3\\\\&\\quad-\\left(-3\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(0) + (6 \\pi) + (12 \\pi) + (0)=18 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722955 :question/problem "Calculate the outward flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x - y^{3})\\,\\mathbf i+(x + 2)\\,\\mathbf j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf i+4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathbf j$ for $0\\leq t\\leq 2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "The curve is traversed once counterclockwise. With the outward normal,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}(P y\\prime-Q x\\prime)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the components along the supplied curve and differentiate its coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y(t)=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\P(x(t),y(t))&=- 64 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\Q(x(t),y(t))&=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2,\\\\x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad y\\prime(t)=4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the flux integrand, keeping the subtraction sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP y\\prime-Q x\\prime&=\\left(- 64 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad-\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 2\\right)\\\\&\\qquad\\cdot\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=- 256 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}+6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\&\\quad{}-12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the other powers, use $\\cos^2t=\\frac{1+\\cos(2t)}2$.\n\nFor terms containing a remaining factor $\\cos t$, use $u=\\sin t$, $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nIntegrate these terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int - 256 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 64 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=\\frac{9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t&=- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the definite integral, the constants cancel. Evaluate each contribution at the two endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 t - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=- 12 \\pi\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=- 12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 64 \\sin^{4}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[\\frac{9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=0\\\\&\\quad-\\left(0\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left[- 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}&=-6\\\\&\\quad-\\left(-6\\right)\\\\&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the contributions gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=(- 12 \\pi) + (0) + (0) + (0)=- 12 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722980 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component:\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=-\\cos t.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both terms and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(-\\cos t\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}- \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ - \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ - \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\sin^2t-\\cos^2t=-\\cos(2t)$ and $-2\\sin t\\cos t=-\\sin(2t)$. Both negative contributions remain in the third component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722984 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=t + 1,\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the scalar and each vector component:\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=1.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both terms and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t^{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(t + 1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 t + 1 \\\\ 3 t^{2} + 2 t \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722988 :question/problem "Find $\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}$, where\n\n$$\nu(t)=t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\qquad \\mathbf f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume $t>0$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}=u^{\\prime}(t)\\mathbf f(t)+u(t)\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nThe scalar is itself a product. Differentiate both factors:\n\n$$\nu^{\\prime}(t)=1\\cdot\\ln t+t\\cdot\\frac{1}{t}=\\ln t+1.\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f^{\\prime}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 t \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both terms and add corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\big(u(t)\\mathbf f(t)\\big)^{\\prime}&=\\big(\\ln t+1\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&\\quad+\\big(t \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\big)\\begin{bmatrix}2 t \\\\ e^{t} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{bmatrix}t^{2} \\left(3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\\\ \\left(\\left(t + 1\\right) \\ln{\\left(t \\right)} + 1\\right) e^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the third component, the two logarithmic terms cancel:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\ln t+1}{t}-\\frac{\\ln t}{t}=\\frac{1}{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722997 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- x + y + \\sin{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(-1,1 - \\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} - 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=1 - \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,1 - \\pi)&=\\langle 1,2\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-1,1 - \\pi)\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle&=0 \\\\ x + 2 y - 1 + 2 \\pi&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722999 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2} - 3 y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(-2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - 2 y^{2} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 4 x y - 3 \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,0)&=\\langle 8,-3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,0)\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle&=0 \\\\ 8 x - 3 y + 16&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 8,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723002 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3 \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{4 y}{3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle \\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle&=0 \\\\ \\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{y}{2} - \\frac{5}{4}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 3,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723025 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x + 2 y + \\sin{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} - 2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 - \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle&=0 \\\\ y - 1&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723029 :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + 2 x y - x - y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(0,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- 2 x + 2 y - 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 x - 1 \\\\ \\nabla f(0,2)&=\\langle 3,-1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(0,2)\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle&=0 \\\\ 3 x - y + 2&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 3,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723081 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf g(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln u \\\\ u^2 \\\\ 1/u\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=\\sqrt{1+t^2}$. Find all three components of $(\\mathbf g\\circ u)'(t)$ as functions of $t$.\n\nWrite\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf g\\circ u)'(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}A(t) \\\\ B(t) \\\\ C(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$A(t)=$ {{A}}\n\n$B(t)=$ {{B}}\n\n$C(t)=$ {{C}}" :question/worked-solution "The inner function is itself a composition. Differentiate the square root and its input $1+t^2$:\n\n$$\nu'(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+t^2}}\\cdot2t=\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+t^2}}.\n$$\n\nThe outer vector derivative is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf g'(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}1/u \\\\ 2u \\\\ -1/u^2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow substitute the inner input and multiply all three components by the inner derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf g\\circ u)'(t)&=\\mathbf g'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+t^2}} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{1+t^2} \\\\ -\\dfrac{1}{1+t^2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+t^2}} \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}\\dfrac{t}{1+t^2} \\\\ 2t \\\\ -\\dfrac{t}{(1+t^2)^{3/2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $A(t)=t/(1+t^2)$, $B(t)=2t$, and $C(t)=-t/(1+t^2)^{3/2}$. These formulas hold for every real $t$, since $u(t)\\geq1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723089 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf g(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\ln u \\\\ u^2 \\\\ 1/u\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=e^{-t}$. Find $(\\mathbf g\\circ u)'(\\ln 2)$." :question/worked-solution "First find the two derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf g'(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}1/u \\\\ 2u \\\\ -1/u^2\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad u'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\nAt $t=\\ln 2$, the input to $\\mathbf g'$ is $u(\\ln 2)=e^{-\\ln 2}=1/2$, while the scalar multiplier is $u'(\\ln 2)=-1/2$. These are different roles in the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf g\\circ u)'(\\ln 2)&=\\mathbf g'(u(\\ln 2))u'(\\ln 2) \\\\\n&=\\mathbf g'(1/2)\\cdot(-1/2) \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot(-1/2) \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1/2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third component is positive because both the reciprocal derivative and the inner derivative are negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723093 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}3u \\\\ u^2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=2t-1$. Find $(\\mathbf f\\circ u)^{\\prime}(t)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the vector component by component with respect to $u$, and differentiate the inner function with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f'(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad u'(t)=2.\n$$\n\nThe chain rule multiplies every component of $\\mathbf f'(u(t))$ by the same scalar $u'(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf f\\circ u)'(t)&=\\mathbf f'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2(2t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2 \\\\\n&=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8t-4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723112 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2 x^{2} - 2 x y + 4 x + y^{2} - 2 y + 2=25$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf v=\\langle 7,-3\\rangle$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=2 x^{2} - 2 x y + 4 x + y^{2} - 2 y + 2$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=25$. A tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=4 x - 2 y + 4\\\\\nF_y&=- 2 x + 2 y - 2\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle - 2 x + 2 y - 2,- 4 x + 2 y - 4\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle&=0\\\\\n- 2 x + 8 y - 2&=0\\\\\n- 2 (x - 4 y + 1)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe condition gives $y=\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}$. Substitution into the original curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{25 x^{2}}{16} + \\frac{25 x}{8} + \\frac{25}{16}&=25\\\\\n\\frac{25 (x - 3) (x + 5)}{16}&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=-5$ or $x=3$. Recovering the other coordinate gives $(-5,-1)$ and $(3,1)$.\n\nThus the qualifying points are $(3,1)$ and $(-5,-1)$. All listed points lie on the curve. The following nonzero tangent vectors check every choice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t(4,5)&=\\langle 0,-10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle=30\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-6,-5)&=\\langle 0,10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle=-30\\\\\n\\mathbf t(2,7)&=\\langle 8,2\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle=50\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-4,-7)&=\\langle -8,-2\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle=-50\\\\\n\\mathbf t(3,1)&=\\langle -6,-14\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-5,-1)&=\\langle 6,14\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 7,-3\\rangle=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723146 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 8 x + y^{2} + 6 y + 10=25$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf v=\\langle 1,0\\rangle$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 2 x y + 8 x + y^{2} + 6 y + 10$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=25$. A tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=4 x + 2 y + 8\\\\\nF_y&=2 x + 2 y + 6\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle 2 x + 2 y + 6,- 4 x - 2 y - 8\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle&=0\\\\\n2 x + 2 y + 6&=0\\\\\n2 (x + y + 3)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe condition gives $y=- x - 3$. Substitution into the original curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2 x + 1&=25\\\\\n(x - 4) (x + 6)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=-6$ or $x=4$. Recovering the other coordinate gives $(-6,3)$ and $(4,-7)$.\n\nThus the qualifying points are $(4,-7)$ and $(-6,3)$. All listed points lie on the curve. The following nonzero tangent vectors check every choice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t(4,-7)&=\\langle 0,-10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-6,3)&=\\langle 0,10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(2,-1)&=\\langle 8,-14\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-4,-3)&=\\langle -8,14\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=-8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(3,-9)&=\\langle -6,-2\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=-6\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-5,5)&=\\langle 6,2\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723163 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $x^{2} + y^{2} - 4 y + 4=25$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf v=\\langle 1,0\\rangle$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2} - 4 y + 4$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=25$. A tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x\\\\\nF_y&=2 y - 4\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle 2 y - 4,- 2 x\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle&=0\\\\\n2 y - 4&=0\\\\\n2 (y - 2)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe condition gives $y=2$. Substitution into the original curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}&=25\\\\\n(x - 5) (x + 5)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=-5$ or $x=5$. Recovering the other coordinate gives $(-5,2)$ and $(5,2)$.\n\nThus the qualifying points are $(5,2)$ and $(-5,2)$. All listed points lie on the curve. The following nonzero tangent vectors check every choice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t(5,2)&=\\langle 0,-10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-5,2)&=\\langle 0,10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(3,6)&=\\langle 8,-6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-3,-2)&=\\langle -8,6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=-8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(4,-1)&=\\langle -6,-8\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=-6\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-4,5)&=\\langle 6,8\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,0\\rangle=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723173 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 4=25$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf v=\\langle 1,1\\rangle$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 4$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=25$. A tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x + 2 y\\\\\nF_y&=2 x + 4 y + 4\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle 2 x + 4 y + 4,- 2 x - 2 y\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle&=0\\\\\n2 y + 4&=0\\\\\n2 (y + 2)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe condition gives $y=-2$. Substitution into the original curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 x + 4&=25\\\\\n(x - 7) (x + 3)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=-3$ or $x=7$. Recovering the other coordinate gives $(-3,-2)$ and $(7,-2)$.\n\nThus the qualifying points are $(7,-2)$ and $(-3,-2)$. All listed points lie on the curve. The following nonzero tangent vectors check every choice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t(7,-2)&=\\langle 10,-10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-3,-2)&=\\langle -10,10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(1,2)&=\\langle 14,-6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle=8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(3,-6)&=\\langle -14,6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle=-8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(9,-5)&=\\langle 2,-8\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle=-6\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-5,1)&=\\langle -2,8\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,1\\rangle=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723174 :question/problem "At which points on the curve $x^{2} - 2 x y - 4 x + 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 4=25$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf v=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=x^{2} - 2 x y - 4 x + 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 4$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=25$. A tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x - 2 y - 4\\\\\nF_y&=- 2 x + 4 y + 4\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle - 2 x + 4 y + 4,- 2 x + 2 y + 4\\rangle\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle&=0\\\\\n2 y&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe condition gives $y=0$. Substitution into the original curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 x + 4&=25\\\\\n(x - 7) (x + 3)&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=-3$ or $x=7$. Recovering the other coordinate gives $(-3,0)$ and $(7,0)$.\n\nThus the qualifying points are $(7,0)$ and $(-3,0)$. All listed points lie on the curve. The following nonzero tangent vectors check every choice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t(7,0)&=\\langle -10,-10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-3,0)&=\\langle 10,10\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle=0\\\\\n\\mathbf t(9,4)&=\\langle 2,-6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle=8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(-5,-4)&=\\langle -2,6\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle=-8\\\\\n\\mathbf t(3,-3)&=\\langle -14,-8\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle=-6\\\\\n\\mathbf t(1,3)&=\\langle 14,8\\rangle,\\qquad \\mathbf t\\cdot \\langle 1,-1\\rangle=6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723198 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 e^{x y}=3 x - 3 y + \\frac{2}{e^{2}} + 9$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=- 3 x + 3 y + 2 e^{x y}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{e^{2}} + 9$, and $f(-1,2)=\\frac{2}{e^{2}} + 9$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 y e^{x y} - 3\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 x e^{x y} + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-1,2)&=2 \\left(2\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right) \\left(2\\right)} - 3\\\\ &=-3 + \\frac{4}{e^{2}}\\\\ f_y(-1,2)&=2 \\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right) \\left(2\\right)} + 3\\\\ &=3 - \\frac{2}{e^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-1,2)=\\left(3 - \\frac{2}{e^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(3 - \\frac{4}{e^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $-\\left(3 - \\frac{2}{e^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(3 - \\frac{4}{e^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-1,2)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-1,2)&=\\left(-3 + \\frac{4}{e^{2}}\\right)\\left(3 - \\frac{2}{e^{2}}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(3 - \\frac{2}{e^{2}}\\right)\\left(-3 + \\frac{4}{e^{2}}\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723201 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}=x - 3 \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=- x + 3 \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and $f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=3 y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 1\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=3 x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)&=-1\\\\ &\\quad - 3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &\\quad +3 \\left(0\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=-4\\\\ f_y(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &\\quad +3 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)\\cdot\\mathbf t(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0)&=\\left(-4\\right)\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(-4\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723207 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}=- x^{2} + x y + 2 e$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-2,e)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x y + 2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=2 e$, and $f(-2,e)=2 e$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x - y + 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x + \\frac{2 x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,e)&=2 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(e\\right) + 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=- e - 2\\\\ f_y(-2,e)&=- \\left(-2\\right) + \\frac{2 \\left(-2\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)}\\\\ &=2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-2,e)=\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(2 + e\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,e)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-2,e)&=\\left(- e - 2\\right)\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(2 - \\frac{4}{e}\\right)\\left(- e - 2\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723210 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $- x e^{y^{2} + 1}=x y - 3 y - 4 + e^{2}$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=- x y - x e^{y^{2} + 1} + 3 y$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=-4 + e^{2}$, and $f(-1,-1)=-4 + e^{2}$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- y - e^{y^{2} + 1}\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 2 x y e^{y^{2} + 1} - x + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-1,-1)&=- \\left(-1\\right) - e^{\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 1}\\\\ &=1 - e^{2}\\\\ f_y(-1,-1)&=- 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\left(-1\\right) e^{\\left(-1\\right)^{2} + 1} - \\left(-1\\right) + 3\\\\ &=4 - 2 e^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-1,-1)=\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i-\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $-\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-1,-1)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-1,-1)&=\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(4 - 2 e^{2}\\right)\\left(1 - e^{2}\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient."}]}
